course: biology
lesson: 195
title: Training a dog with rewards and patience
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A dog does not understand our rules; it learns from what happens right after it does something. If sitting brings a treat, the dog sits more; if jumping up brings attention, it jumps more.
explain: That is the whole secret of good training: catch the dog doing the right thing and reward it at once, so it knows exactly what earned the treat.
explain: Think of a puppy at the door: if everyone squeals and pats it when it jumps up, it has just learnt that jumping works, even if someone also shouts «no!».
explain: Modern trainers use rewards, short clear words that the whole family shares, and lots of patience, because fear and smacking make a dog anxious, not good.
fact: A reward given straight after an action makes that action more likely; this is called positive reinforcement, and it works with dogs, horses, dolphins and even fish.
fact: A clicker makes a short sharp click that marks the exact moment the dog did the right thing, and the treat follows; zoos use clicker training so animals calmly join in health checks.
fact: A puppy jumps up at visitors because jumping got attention before, and even shouting can be a reward, because it is still attention; turning away and rewarding four paws on the floor works better.
fact: A dog pulls on the lead because pulling got it where it wanted to go; it also walks faster than us, so walking nicely beside a person takes practice and rewards.
fact: Old dogs can learn new tricks, and training keeps an older dog's brain busy and happy.
fact: Puppies chew because it is normal for them, especially while their teeth are coming through; swapping the shoe for a chew toy teaches what is allowed.
fact: Coming back when called, called recall, is one of the most important things a dog learns, and a dog should always be rewarded for coming back, even if it took a long time.
fact: In its first few months a puppy should meet many people, animals, sounds and things in a calm way; this is called socialisation, and a dog that met few things when young may be fearful later.
fact: Training with shouting, smacking or fear can make dogs scared or aggressive, so modern trainers use rewards because they work better and are kind.
fact: A dog can learn a way to ask for things, such as ringing a bell at the door to go out for a wee, and some dogs are being taught to press buttons that play recorded words.
fact: A dog does not understand sentences the way we do, so everyone in the family should use the same short word for each command, or the dog gets confused.
exercise: A puppy sits and gets a treat at once. What does it learn? | That sitting brings good things | That treats fall from the sky | That it must never sit again | That the person is angry
exercise: What is it called when a reward makes an action happen more often? | Positive reinforcement | Punishment | Hibernation | Instinct
exercise: Why does a trainer press a clicker the moment the dog does the right thing? | The click marks the exact moment the dog got it right | The click scares the dog into obeying the next command | The click tells the dog to go to sleep straight away | The click wakes the dog up
exercise: Why does a puppy keep jumping up at visitors? | Jumping got it attention before | It wants to bite the visitors | It is too short to see their faces from the floor | It hurt its back legs
exercise: Why can shouting at a jumping puppy make the jumping worse? | Shouting is still attention, which the puppy wanted | Shouting makes its back legs grow stronger | The puppy thinks shouting means food is coming soon | Shouting makes the puppy taller
exercise: Why does a dog pull hard on the lead on every walk? | Pulling got it where it wanted to go | The lead is too short for dogs | Dogs hate walking with people and want to be alone | It wants to go home
exercise: Can an old dog learn a new trick? | Yes, and it keeps its brain busy and happy | No, old dogs cannot learn anything new at all | Only if it is one of the clever puppy breeds | Only the tricks it already knew as a young puppy
exercise: A puppy chews a shoe. What is a good response? | Swap the shoe for a chew toy | Smack the puppy hard | Shout and lock it outside | Rub its nose in the shoe
exercise: What is recall? | Coming back when called | Remembering where a bone is | Barking at the front door | Fetching a ball from the park
exercise: The dog finally comes back in the park after a long time. What should you do? | Reward it for coming back | Tell it off for being slow | Ignore it for the rest of the day | Put it straight on a short lead and shout
exercise: Why should a puppy meet buses, children, cats and umbrellas in its first months? | So it grows up calm about everyday things | So it learns to chase the buses in the street | So it learns to open umbrellas when it rains on walks | So it can choose a favourite child to live with
exercise: What is meeting lots of new things calmly when young called? | Socialisation | Hibernation | Migration | Domestication
exercise: A dog trained with shouting and smacking becomes scared. Why do modern trainers use rewards instead? | Rewards work better and are kind | Rewards make dogs lazy | Smacking is quicker but trainers are soft | Rewards only work on puppies
exercise: A dog learns to ring a bell at the door. What has it learnt? | A way to ask to go outside | To play music | To call the neighbours | To scare away cats
exercise: A family all use different words for sit. What happens? | The dog gets confused | The dog learns four times faster | The dog learns only big words | Nothing, dogs understand all words
exercise: What is the best way to give commands in a family? | Everyone uses the same short word for each command | Everyone makes up their own word, so the dog learns more | Use long sentences | Change the word every week so it does not get bored
test: «stupid mutt pulls like a train, yank him back harder» What works better? | Stop when he pulls and reward him when the lead is loose | Yank harder every time until he learns to stay right next to you | Let him pull wherever he likes | Never walk him again
test: «Nan says you can't teach an old dog new tricks, so Rusty's 11, no point» Is Nan right? | No, old dogs can learn, and it keeps them happy | Yes, old dogs never learn anything new | Yes, but only for cats | No, old dogs can learn, but only how to bark louder
test: «Max ran off for 20 mins then came back, I'm gonna give him a right telling off» What will Max learn from that? | That coming back gets him into trouble | That he should come back faster | Nothing at all | That running off is bad
test: What would happen if you gave the treat five minutes after the dog sat? | The dog would not know what the treat was for | The dog would learn to sit faster next time | The dog would learn to wait exactly five minutes for every treat | The dog would learn to count
test: In a cartoon a dog learns a hard trick when the owner shouts «NO!» very loudly. What is true of real dogs? | Shouting and fear make dogs scared; rewards teach better | Shouting is the best way to teach, because the dog never forgets it | Real dogs only learn when smacked | Real dogs learn only from cartoons
test: «Mum says sit, Dad says sit down, I say bum on floor. why's he not getting it» What is the problem? | The dog needs one word that everyone uses | The dog is too stupid to learn any words | The dog cannot hear | Bum on floor is too short for a dog to understand
test: «puppy wrecked my trainer, why is he so naughty» What is the real reason? | Chewing is normal for puppies, so give him chew toys | He hates you | He is ill and needs to see the vet | He wants new shoes too, because he is jealous of your trainers
test: «why do zoo keepers click at the sea lion lol» What is the reason? | The click marks good behaviour so health checks are calm | The click is a special sea lion word meaning thank you | They are counting fish | It scares sharks away from the pool so the sea lion feels safe
test: Count them: 4 puppies at puppy class each get 3 treats. How many treats is that? | 12 | 7 | 16 | 10
test: Count them: a trainer has two dogs, a pony and a parrot. How many animal legs are there, not counting the trainer? | 14 | 16 | 12 | 10
test: «our dog barks at umbrellas like they're monsters» What might have helped when he was a puppy? | Meeting umbrellas and many other things calmly when young | Never seeing anything new | Being kept inside all year | Being shouted at near umbrellas
test: «she rings that bell then stands at the door, so annoying» What has the dog learned? | A way to ask to go out | To make a noise just for fun | That bells give treats only | To call the cat in from outside
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises positive reinforcement? | A reward which follows an action renders that action more probable | A punishment which follows an action renders that action more probable on every occasion | It is applicable exclusively to young dogs | It operates only upon fish
exam: Identify the principal function of the clicker employed in animal training. | To mark the precise moment at which the desired action was performed | To frighten the animal into compliance by means of a sudden sharp noise | To signal the conclusion of the meal | To summon other animals
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the training of elderly dogs. | Elderly dogs remain capable of learning, and such training benefits them | Elderly dogs are wholly incapable of learning | Training is injurious to dogs exceeding five years of age | Only tricks acquired in youth may be performed
exam: It is given that a dog returns to its owner after a prolonged absence in a public park. Ascertain the response most likely to encourage future returns. | The dog ought to be rewarded upon its return | The dog ought to be reprimanded severely | The dog ought to be ignored entirely | The dog ought to be confined for the day
